# Commands run by the launchd job com.hackios.demo (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.airlib).
#
# The 124 library functions of PortraitFilters that the generated wrapper can run, on the Adreno.
#
# These are air.visible functions: not entry points, and not dispatchable as themselves. Metal
# stitches one into a caller at pipeline time through the ABI in its own metadata, which this
# project does not implement. air_run --library runs the wrapper air_to_nir generates instead:
# argument i from device buffer 0 at byte 16*i, the result into buffer 1 (docs/air.md section 5,
# tools/air/libcheck.py). The argument buffer holds floats, not the usual byte pattern, because
# these modules promise no infinities and no NaNs -- with arbitrary bytes both sides of a
# comparison are outside the contract and a disagreement means nothing.
#
# What this run can establish: that the wrapper's shader dispatches on the hardware and that the
# words it writes are the words our own folder computes on the host. For 21 of these functions
# LLVM on the host agrees with that folding too, so those three readings form a chain. For the
# rest the folder and the Adreno are both ours, and the comparison catches the backend and the
# hardware disagreeing with the folder -- which is how the ir3 u32-to-f32 rounding bug surfaced --
# and nothing more. It is not evidence about Metal's stitching ABI, which is not implemented.

/hackios/bin/air_run --library /System/Library/CoreImage/PortraitFilters.cifilter/portrait_filters.metallib _pf_kickLightKernel_pos
/hackios/bin/air_run --library /System/Library/CoreImage/PortraitFilters.cifilter/portrait_filters.metallib _pf_kickLightKernel_neg
